"{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1353/lan.2014.0031\",\n  \"title\": \"A Statistical Comparison of Written Language and Nonlinguistic Symbol\\n                        Systems\",\n  \"authors\": \"Richard Sproat\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>Are statistical methods useful in distinguishing written language from\\n                        nonlinguistic symbol systems? Some recent articles (Rao et al. 2009a, Lee et\\n                        al. 2010a) have claimed so. Both of these previous articles use measures\\n                        based at least in part on bigram conditional entropy, and subsequent work by\\n                        one of the authors (Rao) has used other entropic measures. In both cases the\\n                        authors have argued that the methods proposed either are useful for\\n                        discriminating between linguistic and nonlinguistic systems (Lee et al.), or\\n                        at least count as evidence of a more ‘inductive’ kind for the status of a\\n                        system (Rao et al.).</jats:p>\\n                  <jats:p>Using a larger set of nonlinguistic and comparison linguistic corpora\\n                        than were used in these and other studies, I show that none of the\\n                        previously proposed methods are useful as published. However, one of the\\n                        measures proposed by Lee and colleagues (2010a) (with a different cut-off\\n                        value) and a novel measure based on repetition turn out to be good measures\\n                        for classifying symbol systems into the two categories. For the two ancient\\n                        symbol systems of interest to Rao and colleagues (2009a) and Lee and\\n                        colleagues (2010a)—Indus Valley inscriptions and Pictish symbols,\\n                        respectively—both of these measures classify them as nonlinguistic,\\n                        contradicting the findings of those previous works.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1353/lan.2014.0031\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2014-06-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1353/lan.2014.0031\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 19,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press (CUP)', 'container_title': 'Language', 'volume': '90', 'issue': '2', 'page': '457-481', 'issn': ['0097-8507', '1535-0665'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1017/cbo9781139519717.003\",\n  \"title\": \"Language as an effective symbol of community\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1017/cbo9781139519717.003\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2013-08-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781139519717.003\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Cambridge University Press', 'container_title': 'Law and Language', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '36-63', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '56', 'prefix': '10.1017'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.15760/etd.6760\",\n  \"title\": \"Nonlinguistic Cognitive Performance and Expressive and Receptive Language Scores in Children with Expressive Language Delay\",\n  \"authors\": \"Deborah Warren\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.15760/etd.6760\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2000-01-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.15760/etd.6760\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"report\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Portland State University Library', 'container_title': '', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': [], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'report', 'member': '6539', 'prefix': '10.15760'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1177/17438721241279052\",\n  \"title\": \"Law: Language, Symbol, Rhetoric\",\n  \"authors\": \"Francis J. Mootz\",\n  \"abstract\": \"<jats:p>Legal positivists regard law as written texts enacted and disseminated to be followed as rules. But this project rests on deeply mistaken conceptions about how language works. No written text stands alone and conveys a “plain meaning.” Law is expressed through language, but language does not exhaust the meaning of the law. Legal language always already draws on the ordinary language of the community, which, in turn, draws on a deeply symbolic realm, which it can never fully subordinate or explain. Rhetoric brings the symbolic substrate to life. Legal actors constantly search for the right words to deliberate with others. There is a resonant world to embrace and experience, but it can never be fully captured, understood, or expressed in our legal texts, which hover precariously above our shared ordinary language, which, in turn, is rooted in constitutive symbols that are sustained through an endless spiral of mediation, translation, and application. Nevertheless, we may reason together in a manner that generates rhetorical knowledge even if legal positivism is a failure.</jats:p>\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1177/17438721241279052\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2024-09-08T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/pdf/10.1177/17438721241279052\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1177/17438721241279052\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 2,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'SAGE Publications', 'container_title': 'Law, Culture and the Humanities', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '', 'issn': ['1743-8721', '1743-9752'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '179', 'prefix': '10.1177'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.4324/9781410602367-18\",\n  \"title\": \"Statistical Learning in Linguistic and Nonlinguistic Domains\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.4324/9781410602367-18\",\n  \"published_date\": \"2013-03-07T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.4324/9781410602367-18\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"book-chapter\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 0,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Psychology Press', 'container_title': 'The Emergence of Language', 'volume': '', 'issue': '', 'page': '377-398', 'issn': [], 'isbn': ['9781410602367'], 'crossref_type': 'book-chapter', 'member': '301', 'prefix': '10.4324'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.1016/0093-934x(92)90110-z\",\n  \"title\": \"Dissociation between linguistic and nonlinguistic gestural systems: A case for compositionality*1\",\n  \"authors\": \"D CORINA\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.1016/0093-934x(92)90110-z\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1992-10-01T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/0093-934x(92)90110-z\",\n  \"source\": \"crossref\",\n  \"updated_date\": \"\",\n  \"categories\": \"journal-article\",\n  \"keywords\": \"\",\n  \"citations\": 105,\n  \"references\": \"\",\n  \"extra\": \"{'publisher': 'Elsevier BV', 'container_title': 'Brain and Language', 'volume': '43', 'issue': '3', 'page': '414-447', 'issn': ['0093-934X'], 'isbn': [], 'crossref_type': 'journal-article', 'member': '78', 'prefix': '10.1016'}\"\n}\n{\n  \"paper_id\": \"10.7551/mitpress/1810.003.0009\",\n  \"title\": \"Evidence from Nonlinguistic Cognitive Tasks\",\n  \"authors\": \"\",\n  \"abstract\": \"\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.7551/mitpress/1810.003.0009\",\n  \"published_date\": \"1997-09-22T00:00:00\",\n  \"pdf_url\": \"\",\n  \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.7551/mi